{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"`rebar review-code --ref <sha> --source attested` accepts both values, forwards them through the\nlibrary, and drops them at the shim. This is not merely unimplemented: `CodeReviewRequest` has a\n`source` field (`gate_dispatch.py:297`) that `gate_source.resolve_gate_handle` consumes\n(`gate_dispatch.py:433`), so the caller's request is actively discarded at the seam.\n\n`llm/code_review/shim.py:127-128` declares `ref`/`source`; the `CodeReviewRequest(...)` literal at\n`:146-158` omits them. `shim.py` never imports `gate_source`, and `_verdict_to_review_result`\n(`:79-104`) never stamps `source`/`verified_at_sha` — while `operations.py:242`,\n`completion.py:488`, `plan_review/__init__.py:473` and `spec_scan.py:69` all do.\n\n`_mcp_llm.py:93-96` tells MCP callers that results carry `source`/`verified_at_sha`/`signable`.\nThat is a false promise on the trust boundary that decides whether a code review can be signed:\n`--source attested` silently reviews the dirty working tree and yields an unsignable result,\nindistinguishable from a successful attested run.\n\nHighest-scoring finding of the audit (validity 1.0, impact 0.92). Both remediation proposers\nconverged on this approach independently.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `ref` and `source` are forwarded into the `CodeReviewRequest` literal\n- [x] `_verdict_to_review_result` stamps `source` and `verified_at_sha`, matching the four sibling producers\n- [x] A behavioural test covers each `source` value, not just the signature change\n- [x] `_mcp_llm.py`'s documented promise is true of the returned result"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786086556449421001,"uuid":"df21453b-f287-4906-8221-9e94047fe9b5"}